commit 07050d92ea2c57a5ec87f0f8a71e9507411a42be
Author: HalcyonOmega <nathanbrown@me.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 20 02:22:48 2026 -0400

    fix: a task drains its owned groups before its completion is published
    
    The interpreter was right and native was wrong, which is the opposite of the
    direction I first assumed. spec.md:2563-2566 and :2609-2610 require task.group to
    join its children before the cancelled task completes, and D-CANCELMODEL1=C at
    :2410-2419 governs the cancel itself. The evaluator already closed and drained
    the group before publishing completion; native cancellation exited the task frame
    and published the completion first, with the fallback close running only after
    the caller had already returned. So a child's own output went missing on the
    native tiers: the interpreter printed the child's settle line and native printed
    nothing.
    
    Task-owned groups now drain before the scheduler publishes completion. The
    ordering rule stays in the Prelude group close where it already lived; AOT and the
    resident host each reach it through their existing adapter, so there is one rule
    and two marshalling calls rather than two orderings.
    
    Cancellation stays structured rather than becoming a hang: the cancel path uses
    the ordinary group close, no extra child cancel is introduced, and the drain ends
    when the child publishes its completion. Finite uncooperative work finishes
    naturally; genuinely unbounded work stays bound by the structured join instead of
    being forcibly detached, which is the ratified shape.

